NAME
    addr - decode a CPU-bus address

SYNOPSIS
    addr <addr>

DESCRIPTION
    Says what a 24-bit CPU-bus address actually reaches: a ROM file offset,
    a RAM offset, or a hardware register. The CPU's view and the ROM file's
    layout are different coordinate systems, and every task that crosses
    between them - comparing against a ROM in a hex editor, writing a patch,
    reading a disassembly someone else produced - needs the translation.
    Doing it by hand means knowing the mapper, which is exactly the detail
    worth not having to remember.

    The decode is the cartridge's own, the same one a real read goes
    through, so it is right for whatever mapper this ROM uses rather than
    assuming LoROM. ROM offsets are reported modulo the ROM's real size,
    matching how an undersized ROM mirrors on hardware.

    Addressable results print a ready-made 'mem' command underneath, since
    looking at the bytes is almost always the next thing wanted. A hardware
    register reports as a register rather than being given a fake offset -
    there is nothing there to point 'mem' at.

    An address the cartridge does not map and the bus does not claim
    reports as unmapped, which is itself an answer: a pointer that decodes
    to nothing is a pointer that was computed wrong.

EXAMPLES
    addr 808000
    addr 7E0DB3
    addr 002100

SEE ALSO
    mem, dump, spaces, vectors, cheat
